body.p:
As discussed in the application as-filed, patient movement during a surgical operation in which navigation is used can present issues. Registration of the patient to a navigation system or a navigated item may include scanning the patient and the navigated item to determine a spatial correlation between the patient and the navigated item which enables a robotic arm to be precisely positioned relative to the patient. See Application as-publishedSVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.150.077[0046]. However, if the patient moves during the surgical operation, the registration may be invalid or inaccurate and the robotic arm may no longer be accurately positioned relative to the patient. Id. The application as-filed then provides a solution by providing a tracking marker on a table on which the patient is positioned on and a tracking marker on the patient (or an anatomical element of the patient). The tracking markers are captured in image or sensor data from which the relative poses of the tracking markers can be determined. Id., at   [0048]. The relative poses can then be compared to determine if the patient has moved relative to the table during the surgical operation. Id. Then, reregistration may occur to account for the movement of the patient and/or the table so that the robotic arm can be precisely positioned relative to the patient.
